window.AMP_ARTICLES = {
"retained-vs-contingent-physician-search": "\n<p>When a hospital or medical group needs a physician, the search model is not a footnote \u2014 it is the search condition. Dedicated and contingent firms both claim they can \u201cfill the role.\u201d Only one model is structurally built to get a prepared candidate to the hiring meeting with a client who is actually ready.</p>\n<p>Adaptive Medical Partners (AMP) treats dedicated investment search as a prepared process: the organization arrives ready to hire with a clear brief; candidates search for the right role; AMP educates, filters, and stages the hiring meeting. Contingent search often looks cheaper on day one and more expensive in missed hires, stalled committees, and half-told stories that leak into the market.</p>\n<h2>What contingent search optimizes for</h2>\n<p>Contingent search typically pays only on placement. That can sound aligned. In practice it rewards speed-to-resume over readiness-to-decide. Multiple firms may work the same role. Candidates hear conflicting stories. Clients receive volume without a shared scorecard. Marketing is thin because nobody owns the narrative long enough to invest in it.</p>\n<p>None of that means contingent is always wrong. For commodity, high-supply roles with a clean, public package, contingent can work. The failure mode appears when the seat is strategic, confidential, or competitive \u2014 exactly when you need a guide.</p>\n<h2>What dedicated investment search should buy you</h2>\n<p>A serious dedicated engagement buys three things that contingent rarely funds well:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Upfront commitment</strong> \u2014 both sides invest; the search gets a real workplan, not a blast.</li>\n<li><strong>Placement accountability</strong> \u2014 process milestones, candidate quality bars, and a defined path to the hiring interview.</li>\n<li><strong>Marketing that protects the brand</strong> \u2014 preview-level public stories, not dossier dumps that let the market decide without you.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>AMP\u2019s hiring preview is intentional: enough signal to attract the right candidates, never enough to replace a conversation with your guide. That \u201cno leaks\u201d discipline is how confidential searches stay confidential while still moving.</p>\n<h2>Fee structure without the fog</h2>\n<p>Organizations should ask any firm to separate <em>search economics</em> from <em>search craft</em>. Upfront fees fund dedicated sourcing, specialty expertise, and outbound that is not optional. Placement fees align to outcome. Marketing support \u2014 creative, job previews, outreach sequences \u2014 should be visible in the workplan, not implied in a slide.</p>\n<p>If a proposal cannot explain what happens in weeks 1\u20133, 4\u20136, and at the first hiring meeting, you are buying hope, not a plan.</p>\n<h2>Candidate experience is a client outcome</h2>\n<p>Physicians compare notes. A chaotic contingent process teaches candidates that your organization is chaotic. A guided dedicated process \u2014 specialty \u2192 region \u2192 controlled job preview \u2192 Tap to Talk \u2192 a recruiting guide \u2014 teaches candidates that someone is steering. AMP\u2019s search model is not decoration; it is operating system: educate the candidate, arrive prepared, meet the client ready to hire.</p>\n<h2>How to choose without theater</h2>\n<p>Compare firms on: specialty depth, confidentiality discipline, marketing craft, time-to-slate quality (not just time-to-first-resume), and whether interest actually lands with a team that monitors responses. AMP routes physician interest into <strong>a recruiting guide</strong> and client intake into <strong>our recruiting team</strong> plus a <strong>hiring guide</strong> path \u2014 destinations with owners, not inboxes that vanish.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Is dedicated always more expensive?</summary><p>Dedicated usually costs more upfront and less in failed cycles. Total cost of vacancy plus committee time often dwarfs fee deltas.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Can AMP help if we are unsure which model fits?</summary><p>Yes. Start at the client door, share facility type and specialty, and request a meeting. You will see a hiring preview \u2014 not a contract dump \u2014 before any commitment theater.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Where should physicians go from here?</summary><p>Use the physician door to search roles by specialty and region, or read <a href=\"#blog/evaluate-obgyn-opportunities\" data-go=\"blog/evaluate-obgyn-opportunities\">how to evaluate OB/GYN opportunities</a>.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><em>Next steps:</em> <button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m hiring / client</button> \u00b7 <button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"amp-score\">AMP Score</button></p>\n\n<h2>Marketing craft is part of the fee, not a freebie</h2>\n<p>Dedicated investment search that underinvests in marketing forces candidates to discover roles through rumor. AMP treats public job pages as practice-first previews: cinematic enough to stop the scroll, disciplined enough to avoid leaking the full map. That means lifestyle heroes, practice-first headlines, and controlled reveals \u2014 then a recruiting guide. Contingent blasts often invert this: maximum detail, minimum narrative ownership, and five firms saying five different things about the same seat.</p>\n<p>When organizations evaluate proposals, ask who writes the preview, who approves it, and how quickly a role can appear in a Specialty \u2192 Region \u2192 Jobs path without exposing non-public economics. If the firm cannot show a practice-first preview with a falsifiable week structure, they may not have a point of view \u2014 only a database.</p>\n<h2>Operational destinations beat vanity metrics</h2>\n<p>\u201cWe sent 40 candidates\u201d is not a KPI if none were interview-ready. Ask where responses live. AMP\u2019s site experience is explicit: physician interest reaches a recruiting guide; client meetings land in our recruiting team client intake with a hiring guide handoff. Interest lands with a recruiting guide or hiring guide — owned next steps, not a vanishing inbox.</p>\n<p>Finally, remember the recruiting model. Candidates are searching. Clients arrive ready to hire with a clear brief. AMP prepares both sides of the search. Dedicated economics fund that guide work. Contingent economics often fund a race. Choose the model that matches the difficulty of the role you are asking someone to take.</p>\n",
"evaluate-obgyn-opportunities": "\n<p>OB/GYN opportunities are easy to romanticize and hard to pressure-test. Brochures lead with \u201ccollegial,\u201d \u201cbusy from day one,\u201d and a city nickname. Physicians need a different checklist: schedule reality, OR/clinic mix, call burden, income structure, and whether the organization can explain those things on a short call without dumping a 40-page packet into your inbox.</p>\n<p>AMP\u2019s approach is practice-first headlines and \u22645 bullets on public pages \u2014 then Tap to Talk with a named guide. A strong public preview is intentionally sharp: a falsifiable weekday claim and a public income band \u2014 with the curve, stack, and facility specifics gated.</p>\n<h2>Start with the week, not the zip code</h2>\n<p>Ask: What does Tuesday look like? When does clinic end? Who covers labor when you are not on? How often do \u201cshort days\u201d become long days? A lifestyle hero photo is useful mood; it is not evidence. A preview that states when the week ends is valuable precisely because it makes a falsifiable claim you can verify on a call.</p>\n<h2>Separate income band from income story</h2>\n<p>Public bands (like from $812,000) help you decide whether to spend 90 seconds. They should not pretend to be the full stack. Commencement, CME, PTO, malpractice structure, and wRVU curves belong in a confidential conversation \u2014 not on a page that competitors and patients can scrape. That is not secrecy for its own sake; it is how dedicated investment searches protect both sides of the search.</p>\n<h2>A five-point evaluation checklist</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Schedule integrity</strong> \u2014 written clinic hours vs. cultural hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Call architecture</strong> \u2014 frequency, backup, midwife/APP support.</li>\n<li><strong>Surgical access</strong> \u2014 block time, robotics, referral patterns.</li>\n<li><strong>Income mechanics</strong> \u2014 guarantee length, conversion, downside protection.</li>\n<li><strong>Decision path</strong> \u2014 who interviews, how fast, what \u201cyes\u201d requires.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>How AMP keeps you from drowning in PDFs</h2>\n<p>When you tap through Specialty \u2192 Region \u2192 Jobs \u2192 View, you get a world-class preview, not a dossier. Interest routes to a named guide such as Amy Myers (call/text, email with CC inquire@) and reaches a <strong>recruiting guide</strong>. You are never left in a dead-end form.</p>\n<h2>Red flags that look like green flags</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>\u201cCompetitive compensation\u201d with no band and no call offer.</li>\n<li>Facility names everywhere, schedule nowhere.</li>\n<li>Pressure to decide before a guide conversation.</li>\n<li>Multiple recruiters telling different versions of the same seat.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Should I apply on the hospital site and through AMP?</summary><p>If AMP is representing the search, let your guide coordinate. Dual paths create crossed wires and weaken confidentiality.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Is Midwest code for \u201crural only\u201d?</summary><p>No. Midwest includes community employed models with strong lifestyle upside. Read our <a href=\"#blog/midwest-lifestyle-for-physicians\" data-go=\"blog/midwest-lifestyle-for-physicians\">Midwest lifestyle guide</a>.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m a physician \u2014 start a search</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">Browse physician roles</button></p>\n\n<h2>Translate brochure language into clinical reality</h2>\n<p>\u201cBusy from day one\u201d can mean healthy referrals \u2014 or an understaffed call pool. \u201cSupportive administration\u201d can mean responsive leadership \u2014 or silence until budget season. Write down the claim, then ask your AMP guide what evidence would confirm it. On a world-class preview page you should see at most five bullets that earn a call, not twenty that pretend to replace one.</p>\n<p>For employed OB/GYN seats, clarify induction policies, backup for complex obstetrics, GYN OR access, and how APP collaboration actually works on a Tuesday afternoon. Then ask how compensation changes after any guarantee period. Public bands create permission to engage; private curves create permission to decide.</p>\n<h2>Use the physician funnel as a decision scaffold</h2>\n<p>AMP\u2019s physician path is Specialty \u2192 Region \u2192 Jobs list \u2192 Job View \u2192 Tap to Talk / Text / Email / chatbot \u2192 confirmation in a recruiting guide. That sequence is deliberate. It prevents the common failure mode where a physician bookmarks twelve PDFs and still cannot compare weeks. If you are comparing Midwest options, open a practice-first preview that earns a call, then ask your guide what else in the specialty corridor matches your non-negotiables.</p>\n<p>Bring your partner into the early filters: region, school constraints, visa or license timing, and tolerance for call. Hiring meetings go better when the search was honest from the start.</p>\n",
"amp-score-explained": "\n<p>AMP Score is Adaptive Medical Partners\u2019 way of making \u201cfit\u201d less mystical. Physicians and organizations both drown in adjectives. AMP Score turns fit into measurable dimensions: lifestyle, practice design, compensation clarity, culture signals, and readiness for a hiring meeting.</p>\n<p>It is not a public ranking of hospitals. It is not a credit score for doctors. It is a guided explainer and intake lens that helps AMP route people faster \u2014 into the right specialty path, the right conversation, and the right destination system (<strong>our recruiting team</strong> / <strong>a hiring guide</strong>).</p>\n<h2>Why scoring belongs in recruiting</h2>\n<p>Without a scorecard, every opportunity feels like a vibe check. Committees disagree. Candidates ghost. Guides waste cycles. A simple scored model creates shared language: \u201cThis is a 78 on schedule integrity and a 62 on compensation clarity \u2014 worth a call, not a blind flyout.\u201d</p>\n<h2>The dimensions (interactive explainer)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lifestyle fit</strong> \u2014 weekday endings, call load, commute, community pace.</li>\n<li><strong>Practice design</strong> \u2014 panel support, OR access, team model.</li>\n<li><strong>Compensation clarity</strong> \u2014 whether the story can be told cleanly on a short call.</li>\n<li><strong>Culture &amp; leadership</strong> \u2014 interview signal quality, not poster slogans.</li>\n<li><strong>Interview readiness</strong> \u2014 is the client prepared to meet a prepared candidate?</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How physicians should use it</h2>\n<p>Treat AMP Score as a flashlight, not a verdict. Run the interactive experience on this site, then talk to a guide. If you are exploring OB/GYN in the Midwest, pair your score intuition with a practice-first job preview and a Tap to Talk \u2014 details stay gated for a confidential call.</p>\n<h2>How organizations should use it</h2>\n<p>If your role scores low on compensation clarity, fix the brief before you buy more sourcing. Dedicated investment search cannot market fog. AMP\u2019s client funnel shows a hiring preview (upfront + placement + marketing) and then requests a meeting that lands with a hiring guide \u2014 so business development and delivery share one story.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Is AMP Score public for every job?</summary><p>No. Public pages stay preview-level. Scores inform guide conversations and guide conversations.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Does a high score mean I should say yes?</summary><p>No. It means the conversation is worth having. Decisions still belong to you and your family.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"amp-score\">Try the AMP Score</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"education\">Back to Why AMP</button></p>\n\n<h2>How the interactive score works on this site</h2>\n<p>The AMP Score page on this site is an explainer plus a simple interactive experience. You answer a few prompts about schedule sensitivity, compensation clarity needs, and culture priorities. The experience returns an illustrative score \u2014 for example a strong 78 \u2014 and invites a next step into the physician or client door. It is intentionally not a black-box AI verdict. It is a conversation starter aligned to AMP\u2019s guide model.</p>\n<p>In practice, the score helps AMP guide the conversation. A candidate chasing schedule integrity should not be delayed by a role that cannot state clinic end times. A client with fuzzy economics should get a briefing workshop before a national campaign. Score gaps become workplans.</p>\n<h2>What to do after your score</h2>\n<p>Pair AMP Score with dedicated-vs-contingent guidance and a practice-first job preview. When you finish the interactive experience, take one action: talk to a guide or request a meeting.</p>\n",
"midwest-lifestyle-for-physicians": "\n<p>\u201cMidwest lifestyle\u201d is either a clich\u00e9 or a competitive advantage, depending on whether anyone will define it. For physicians, lifestyle is not a lake photo. It is whether Monday\u2013Friday actually ends, whether schools and partners\u2019 careers work, and whether the clinic\u2019s culture matches the postcard.</p>\n<p>AMP leans into lifestyle heroes on job pages precisely because physicians decide with their whole life \u2014 then we refuse to pretend a photo replaces a package conversation. Strong Midwest OB/GYN previews lead with a weekday that actually ends and a public income band. That is lifestyle with a number attached, still gated where it should be.</p>\n<h2>What Midwest often gets right</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Community employed models with clearer panels than coastal auction markets.</li>\n<li>Shorter commutes and lower housing friction in many metros and mid-size cities.</li>\n<li>Space \u2014 literal and cultural \u2014 for a life outside clinic.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What you must still pressure-test</h2>\n<p>Winter is real. Specialist density varies. \u201cFriendly\u201d can mean supportive or mean conflict-avoidant. Ask your guide for schedule integrity stories, not tourism boards. AMP\u2019s physician funnel \u2014 Specialty \u2192 Region \u2192 Jobs \u2192 View \u2014 is built so Midwest is a filter, not a fog machine.</p>\n<h2>Family and dual-career reality</h2>\n<p>Bring partner career constraints early. A hiring meeting that ignores dual-career logistics is a delayed no. Guides who route interest into <strong>a recruiting guide</strong> can coordinate confidentially without broadcasting your search.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>What should a public Midwest preview include?</summary><p>A practice-first headline, \u22645 bullets, a named guide contact, and no full dossier dump \u2014 enough to earn a call, not enough to replace one.</p></details>\n<details><summary>How do I start?</summary><p><button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">Enter the physician path</button> and choose OB/GYN \u2192 Midwest.</p></details>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Building a Midwest shortlist without self-sabotage</h2>\n<p>Create three buckets: must-have schedule truths, income floor for a serious conversation, and family constraints. Run AMP\u2019s region filter, then open previews with lifestyle heroes and practice-first headlines. If a page leads with a hospital brand and hides the week, invert your attention \u2014 ask for the week first.</p>\n<p>Community size is not morality. Some physicians thrive in mid-size referral hubs; others want metro amenities with a community employed contract. Midwest contains both. What it often shares is a cultural willingness to talk plainly about call and panel \u2014 if you have a guide who knows how to ask.</p>\n<h2>From lifestyle photo to a recruiting guide</h2>\n<p>When a preview hits your bar, do not stall in private browser tabs. Call, text, or email Amy Myers with CC to inquire@, or use the Ask a guide chat. Confirmation screens in this site show a recruiting guide so you can see the operational landing zone. Lifestyle decisions deserve operational seriousness.</p>\n<p>Also read AMP Score and the interview-prep-without-leaks guide before hiring meetings. Arrive as a prepared candidate. That is how Midwest opportunities stop being postcards and start being choices.</p>\n\n<h2>Practical next step</h2>\n<p>If this article clarified your thinking, take one concrete step on this site within five minutes: open the physician door, open the client door, or run AMP Score. When you are ready, talk with a recruiting guide or hiring guide.</p>\n",
"what-hospitals-should-ask-retained-firm": "\n<p>Before you hire a physician search firm, interview them like a strategic partner \u2014 because they will speak for your organization in the market. Soft questions get soft answers. Use the list below. AMP expects to answer all of them.</p>\n<h2>Twelve questions that separate guides from vendors</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Who owns the search day to day, and what else are they carrying?</li>\n<li>What does week one look like in writing?</li>\n<li>How do you market without leaking confidential details?</li>\n<li>What is your definition of a qualified slate \u2014 count or quality?</li>\n<li>How do candidates enter your response system (name the system)?</li>\n<li>How do client meetings get scheduled and briefed?</li>\n<li>What is included in upfront vs placement vs marketing?</li>\n<li>How do you handle competing specialties or shifting leadership?</li>\n<li>Show a redacted example of a job preview you are proud of.</li>\n<li>What happens when a search stalls at committee?</li>\n<li>How do you coordinate with in-house recruitment?</li>\n<li>Where does interest live after a form submit \u2014 inbox or operations hub?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>AMP\u2019s honest answers point to destinations: physician interest \u2192 <strong>a recruiting guide</strong>; client intake \u2192 <strong>our recruiting team</strong> + <strong>a hiring guide</strong>. If a firm cannot name an operational destination, they are selling PDFs.</p>\n<h2>Dedicated preview vs contract dump</h2>\n<p>Your first meeting should clarify economics at a preview level: upfront commitment, placement alignment, marketing support. You should not need a 30-page MSA to understand the process. AMP\u2019s client funnel is built that way — preview first, details on the call.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>How do we start with AMP?</summary><p>Take the client door: Facility type \u2192 Specialty \u2192 Dedicated preview \u2192 Request meeting.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Do you work only dedicated?</summary><p>Ask on the meeting. This site emphasizes dedicated search craft because confidential, strategic seats need guides.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m hiring \u2014 start client path</button></p>\n\n<h2>Align leadership before you invest with anyone</h2>\n<p>The fastest way to burn dedicated fees is an unclear brief. Chief medical officers, service line leaders, and finance must agree on schedule, income story, and what \u201cgood\u201d looks like in a slate. AMP\u2019s client path \u2014 Facility type \u2192 Specialty \u2192 Dedicated preview \u2192 Request meeting \u2014 is designed to surface misalignment early, while the conversation is still a meeting request rather than a stalled national campaign.</p>\n<p>Ask how the firm will brief candidates for hiring meetings without oversharing. No-leaks discipline protects your brand in the physician community. Physicians talk. Overexposure of unfinished economics teaches the market that your process is unfinished.</p>\n<h2>Hiring guide and delivery should share a spine</h2>\n<p>Business development that sells one story while delivery runs another creates distrust. AMP\u2019s confirmation UI shows client intake in our recruiting team and a hiring guide path. When you evaluate firms, ask to see the handoff. If marketing, sourcing, and sales and delivery teams cannot point to the same destination system, you will feel that fracture in week four.</p>\n<p>Use Why AMP, this checklist, and a request-meeting form to start cleanly. Dedicated investment search is a guided process. Inspect the guide\u2019s kit before you commit.</p>\n",
"candidate-interview-prep-without-leaks": "\n<p>Interview prep fails in two opposite ways: candidates walk in cold, or someone forwards them an internal packet that was never meant to leave the building. AMP\u2019s no-leaks standard exists so you can be sharp without turning confidential client material into public lore.</p>\n<h2>What good prep includes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Your story in 90 seconds: training, what you want in a week, what you will not trade away.</li>\n<li>Practice-first questions (schedule, call, team) ahead of city trivia.</li>\n<li>Compensation questions that invite structure, not poker tells.</li>\n<li>Clarity on timeline and decision partners at home.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What stays gated</h2>\n<p>Named facility deep-dives, non-public financials, and anything marked confidential stay on the call with your guide and the hiring meeting \u2014 not in group chats, not on public job pages. Public previews exist to start the search, not to finish the map.</p>\n<h2>How AMP routes you</h2>\n<p>Tap to Talk / Text / Email / chatbot all converge on <strong>a recruiting guide</strong> for the owner recruiter. That is how prep stays coordinated: one thread, one guide, one recruiting model.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Can I see the full package before a call?</summary><p>You will see a controlled preview publicly. Full stacks are shared in confidential conversation \u2014 by design.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Who do I contact about a role I liked?</summary><p>Use the named guide on the job preview, or Mike Freeman \u00b7 972-532-0355 \u00b7 mfreeman@adaptivemedicalpartners.com \u00b7 CC inquire@adaptivemedicalpartners.com</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">Browse physician roles</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"chat\" data-trail=\"1\">Talk with AMP</button></p>\n\n<h2>Practice questions that sound senior</h2>\n<p>Instead of \u201cWhat\u2019s the comp?\u201d try \u201cWalk me through how a strong first-year physician succeeds economically here after any guarantee.\u201d Instead of \u201cIs the call bad?\u201d try \u201cWhen clinic runs long, who absorbs labor coverage, and how often does that happen in a typical month?\u201d Senior questions invite structure. Structure is what confidential conversations are for.</p>\n<p>Rehearse your non-negotiables aloud with your guide. AMP can pressure-test them against the preview you saw publicly without emailing you a forbidden packet. That is the point of controlled reveal.</p>\n<h2>After the interview</h2>\n<p>Send a concise thank-you through your recruiter of record. Do not freelance confidential details into thank-you notes. Log questions that remain open and ask AMP to close them before you invest in a second visit. Interest and follow-ups still route through a recruiting guide so the owner recruiter keeps the thread coherent.</p>\n<p>If you are early and only browsing, stay in Education and Search until a preview earns a Tap to Talk. The search advances in stages. Skipping stages is how people get lost \u2014 or how leaks start.</p>\n\n<h2>Practical next step</h2>\n<p>If this article clarified your thinking, take one concrete step on this site within five minutes: open the physician door, open the client door, or run AMP Score. When you are ready, talk with a recruiting guide or hiring guide.</p>\n",
"physician-retention-3-year-kpi": "\n<p>Most physician recruiting dashboards still worship a single idol: time-to-fill. Fill the seat. Close the req. Move on. That metric is not wrong \u2014 vacancies hurt patients and burn out the physicians who remain \u2014 but it is incomplete. The retention question is different: <strong>did they stay?</strong></p>\n<p>Adaptive Medical Partners publishes a public proof point from our About language: <strong>87% retention at three years</strong>. That is not a brochure adjective. It is the KPI that tells a hospital whether the hiring meeting produced a lasting partner or a expensive revolving door. In retention terms: placement is making the hire; retention is staying in the role.</p>\n<h2>Why \u201cfilled\u201d is a soft finish line</h2>\n<p>A filled role can hide a failed match. The physician who leaves at month fourteen takes institutional memory, referral relationships, and team trust with them. Committees restart. Locums spend spikes. Candidates in the market hear that your organization churns. Contingent firms that optimize for speed-to-resume rarely own that second-order damage. Dedicated guides should \u2014 because their economics and craft are supposed to fund fit, not just volume.</p>\n<p>AMP\u2019s story is explicit. Candidates are <strong>searching for the right role</strong>. Clients are <strong>ready to hire with a clear brief</strong> while AMP does the search work. If the wait was for the wrong candidate, the work was wasted. Retention is how you know the wait was worth it.</p>\n<h2>Three-year retention as an operating system</h2>\n<p>Three years is long enough to clear the honeymoon and short enough to be honest. It covers schedule reality versus brochure promises, income curve versus guarantee theater, culture versus posters, and family logistics versus city nicknames. When AMP says 87% are still there at three years, we are saying the dedicated search produced durable fit \u2014 not that every search was easy.</p>\n<p>Organizations evaluating firms should ask:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>How do you define retention (employed physician still in role / system)?</li>\n<li>What inputs do you pressure-test before a hiring meeting?</li>\n<li>How does marketing stay preview-level so the market does not decide for you?</li>\n<li>Where do responses live after interest \u2014 a named destination or a vanishing inbox?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>AMP\u2019s honest answers point to destinations: physician interest \u2192 <strong>a recruiting guide</strong>; client meetings \u2192 <strong>our recruiting team</strong> + <strong>a hiring guide</strong>. Retention craft without operational ownership is a slide, not a system.</p>\n<h2>Retention, interview efficiency, and rural reality</h2>\n<p>Retention does not live alone. AMP also cites <strong>1.7 average interviews per successful placement</strong> and partnerships with <strong>700+ rural hospitals / FQHCs / critical access facilities</strong>, with <strong>16 years</strong> of tenure since 2010. Together those numbers describe a guide model: prepare candidates, do not waste hiring meetings, know community-fit corridors, and stay long enough to learn what lasts.</p>\n<p>Read the companion pillars on <a href=\"#blog/interview-to-placement-efficiency\" data-go=\"blog/interview-to-placement-efficiency\">interview-to-placement efficiency</a> and <a href=\"#blog/rural-fqhc-critical-access-recruiting\" data-go=\"blog/rural-fqhc-critical-access-recruiting\">rural / FQHC recruiting</a>. Retention is the north face; those articles are the ridges that support it.</p>\n<h2>What hospitals should change this quarter</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Score stay risk in the brief</strong> \u2014 schedule integrity, call architecture, dual-career constraints, leadership responsiveness.</li>\n<li><strong>Refuse dossier dumps in public</strong> \u2014 preview enough to attract the right searchers; gate economics on a call.</li>\n<li><strong>Demand slate quality over resume count</strong> \u2014 interview-ready beats \u201cwe sent forty.\u201d</li>\n<li><strong>Align BD and delivery</strong> \u2014 if sales promises one path and recruiting delivers another, retention collapses.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Physician lens: why stay rates protect you too</h2>\n<p>If you are searching for the right role, a firm obsessed only with fill speed may rush you into a Tuesday that never ends. Ask how they talk about three-year outcomes. Ask for a controlled preview \u2014 practice-first, \u22645 bullets, package on a short call \u2014 then Tap to Talk. Soft CTAs beat hard pressure.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Is 87% retention a guarantee for my search?</summary><p>No public stat is a personal guarantee. It is AMP\u2019s reported three-year retention proof from About language \u2014 evidence of craft, not a contract clause on this page.</p></details>\n<details><summary>How is this different from time-to-fill marketing?</summary><p>Time-to-fill asks how fast the seat closed. Retention asks whether the close was worth it three years later. Serious organizations track both.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Where should hiring leaders go next?</summary><p>Take the client door for a hiring preview and meeting request to our recruiting team + hiring guide, or read <a href=\"#blog/retained-vs-contingent-physician-search\" data-go=\"blog/retained-vs-contingent-physician-search\">dedicated investment vs contingent</a>.</p></details>\n<details><summary>What about candidates comparing OB/GYN roles?</summary><p>Start at the physician door \u2014 then talk to a recruiting guide such as Amy Myers. Full stacks stay gated for a confidential call.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m hiring \u2014 hold a clear brief</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m a physician \u2014 search for the right role</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"education\">Why AMP</button></p>\n<h2>Retention language without the fog</h2>\n<p>Guides do not measure success only by whether someone touched the hire milestone. They measure whether the physician can stay and succeed in role. AMP\u2019s 87% at three years is that idea in a number. Pair it with 1.7 interview efficiency and 700+ community partnerships, and you get a recruiting narrative that stays honest — no invented towns, no dossier leaks.</p>\n<p>When this article clarifies your thinking, take one step: request a meeting, open the physician path, or run AMP Score. Interest should land with a team that monitors it \u2014 on this site, that is our recruiting team and hiring guide.</p>\n",
"interview-to-placement-efficiency": "\n<p>\u201cWe interviewed twelve people\u201d is not a flex if eleven were never interview-ready. Adaptive Medical Partners cites a sharper efficiency story from our About language: <strong>1.7 average interviews per successful placement</strong>. In retention terms, that means prepared candidates arrive ready to hire \u2014 AMP does not waste the client\u2019s hiring meetings on candidates who were never going to commit.</p>\n<p>Interview-to-placement efficiency is the twin of retention. AMP also reports <strong>87% retention at three years</strong>, partnerships with <strong>700+ rural hospitals / FQHCs / critical access facilities</strong>, and <strong>16 years</strong> since 2010. Efficiency without stay rates is a clever funnel; together they describe a guide model.</p>\n<h2>What 1.7 actually buys a hospital</h2>\n<p>Every interview consumes CMO time, panel coordination, travel or virtual theater, and emotional bandwidth for the service line. Inflated interview counts often signal fuzzy briefs, spray-and-pray sourcing, or contingent races where five firms send five versions of the same seat. A 1.7 average says the hard work happened upstream: education, filtering, and staging \u2014 before anyone sits down for the hiring meeting.</p>\n<p>Clients on AMP\u2019s path are <strong>ready to hire with a clear brief</strong> while AMP does the search work. Candidates are <strong>searching for the right role</strong>. The guide\u2019s job is to make the wait short in wasted meetings, not short in diligence.</p>\n<h2>Efficiency is not ruthlessness</h2>\n<p>Low interview counts can be a red flag if they mean the firm hid options or pressured a yes. Healthy efficiency looks like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A written week-one workplan and specialty scorecard</li>\n<li>Preview-level public marketing that attracts the right searchers</li>\n<li>Confidential package conversations before flyouts</li>\n<li>Interview prep without leaks</li>\n<li>Responses landing in a named system a team monitors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>AMP\u2019s physician funnel \u2014 Specialty \u2192 Region \u2192 Jobs \u2192 Preview \u2192 Tap to Talk / Text / Email / Chat \u2192 <strong>a recruiting guide</strong> \u2014 is built to educate before the hiring meeting. The client funnel \u2014 Facility \u2192 Specialty \u2192 Dedicated preview \u2192 Request meeting \u2192 <strong>our recruiting team</strong> + <strong>a hiring guide</strong> \u2014 is built so leadership waits with a brief worth defending.</p>\n<h2>How dedicated search craft funds fewer, better meetings</h2>\n<p>Dedicated economics pay for outbound that is not optional, specialty expertise, and marketing craft. Contingent economics often pay for a race. Read <a href=\"#blog/retained-vs-contingent-physician-search\" data-go=\"blog/retained-vs-contingent-physician-search\">dedicated investment vs contingent</a> for the model comparison. The 1.7 story is what dedicated should feel like operationally: not endless first dates.</p>\n<p>Pacific-style closing-ratio boasts are useful market language; AMP\u2019s interview efficiency is more precise for committee calendars. Do not invent competitor numbers \u2014 stick to AMP\u2019s About stats and ask every firm for their definition of \u201cinterview\u201d and \u201csuccessful placement.\u201d</p>\n<h2>Candidate experience is part of efficiency</h2>\n<p>Physicians ghost processes that waste their evenings. A guided practice-first preview (falsifiable week, public income band where appropriate) lets a searcher decide whether 90 seconds of interest is warranted \u2014 without a dossier dump. Prep stays ethical: practice questions, non-negotiables, timeline clarity \u2014 not confidential packets. See <a href=\"#blog/candidate-interview-prep-without-leaks\" data-go=\"blog/candidate-interview-prep-without-leaks\">interview prep without leaks</a>.</p>\n<h2>Questions to ask any firm about interview math</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>What counts as an interview in your 1.x metric?</li>\n<li>How do you prevent duplicate stories from multiple recruiters?</li>\n<li>What disqualifies a candidate before a hiring meeting?</li>\n<li>Where do candidate and client responses live after the form?</li>\n<li>How do you connect interview efficiency to three-year retention?</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Does 1.7 mean AMP only presents two candidates ever?</summary><p>It means average interviews per successful placement \u2014 a process efficiency claim from About language \u2014 not a promise that every search has a two-person slate.</p></details>\n<details><summary>How does this relate to 87% retention?</summary><p>Prepared meetings should produce better matches. Retention is the three-year check that efficiency was not just speed theater. Read the <a href=\"#blog/physician-retention-3-year-kpi\" data-go=\"blog/physician-retention-3-year-kpi\">retention pillar</a>.</p></details>\n<details><summary>I\u2019m hiring \u2014 what\u2019s the soft next step?</summary><p><button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">Hold the right role \u2014 client path</button> for facility \u2192 specialty \u2192 hiring preview \u2192 meeting request to our recruiting team + hiring guide.</p></details>\n<details><summary>I\u2019m a physician searching \u2014 where do I start?</summary><p><button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">Enter the physician search</button> or open a job preview from the physician path.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">Request a dedicated conversation</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">Browse physician roles</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"education\">Why AMP</button></p>\n<h2>Put the efficiency story to work</h2>\n<p>AMP repeats <strong>1.7 / 87% / 700+ / 16 years</strong> from About language — no invented extras. When you finish this article, take one action in under five minutes: request a meeting, open the physician path, or talk with a guide.</p>\n",
"rural-fqhc-critical-access-recruiting": "\n<p>Community hospitals, FQHCs, and critical access facilities do not lose physicians for mysterious reasons. They lose them when the week was mis-sold, the call pool was fiction, the town was a postcard instead of a plan, or the firm treated rural as a discount channel. Adaptive Medical Partners\u2019 About language includes a scale proof that matters here: partnerships with <strong>700+ rural hospitals / FQHCs / critical access facilities</strong>, alongside <strong>87% retention at three years</strong>, <strong>1.7 average interviews per successful placement</strong>, and <strong>16 years</strong> since 2010.</p>\n<p>This pillar is about that corridor \u2014 without inventing hospital names or towns. Region-level lifestyle and practice-first previews beat fake local search visibility. Candidates are <strong>searching for the right role</strong>; clients are <strong>ready to hire with a clear brief</strong> while AMP does the work. Community seats are real seats. They deserve dedicated search craft, not spray.</p>\n<h2>What \u201crural / FQHC / CAH\u201d actually demands</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Schedule honesty</strong> \u2014 weekday endings, backup, APP collaboration, obstetrics coverage where relevant.</li>\n<li><strong>Mission without martyrdom</strong> \u2014 FQHC purpose is a magnet; burnout theater is not.</li>\n<li><strong>Family logistics</strong> \u2014 partner careers, schools, housing, distance to tertiary care.</li>\n<li><strong>Income clarity on a call</strong> \u2014 public bands where appropriate; curves gated.</li>\n<li><strong>Confidentiality</strong> \u2014 many community searches cannot survive a dossier leak.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>AMP\u2019s hiring preview \u2014 upfront + placement + marketing \u2014 exists so organizations can fund real outbound and preview-level creative without dumping full economics onto the open web. Meeting requests land in <strong>our recruiting team</strong> with a <strong>hiring guide</strong> path so business development and delivery share one spine.</p>\n<h2>Why 700+ partnerships matter</h2>\n<p>“700+” tells searchers AMP has pattern recognition across community settings — not that every facility should be named on a blog. Pair the number with retention and interview efficiency for a coherent story: candidates searching for the right role, clients ready to hire with a clear brief, AMP doing the work.</p>\n<h2>Practical recruiting playbook for community seats</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the Tuesday before you buy a national campaign.</li>\n<li>Decide what is public preview versus call-gated (practice-first headline, \u22645 bullets, economics on a call).</li>\n<li>Brief leadership on interview readiness \u2014 who interviews, how fast, what \u201cyes\u201d requires.</li>\n<li>Route every physician tap to our recruiting team Responses with an owner recruiter.</li>\n<li>Measure stay risk alongside time-to-slate quality.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Candidate guidance without romanticizing \u201crural\u201d</h2>\n<p>Midwest and other regions contain mid-size hubs and true critical access settings. Neither is morally superior. Pressure-test commute, call, panel support, and dual-career reality. Use Education posts like <a href=\"#blog/midwest-lifestyle-for-physicians\" data-go=\"blog/midwest-lifestyle-for-physicians\">Midwest lifestyle</a> and <a href=\"#blog/evaluate-obgyn-opportunities\" data-go=\"blog/evaluate-obgyn-opportunities\">evaluating OB/GYN opportunities</a>, then enter the physician door. Soft next step: talk to a guide before you fall in love with a lake photo.</p>\n<h2>FQHC and critical access specifics</h2>\n<p>FQHCs need recruiters who understand sliding-fee culture, grant rhythms, and provider wellness \u2014 not only RVU mythology. Critical access facilities need honesty about transfer patterns, ED coverage expectations, and community visibility (you will be known at the grocery store). AMP\u2019s guide model is built for those conversations; contingent blasts often are not.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Will AMP list my hospital on this page?</summary><p>No. Public education stays facility-agnostic. Partnerships are cited at the 700+ aggregate level from About language.</p></details>\n<details><summary>How do retention and rural fit connect?</summary><p>Community roles punish mismatch faster. 87% three-year retention is the stay-rate proof; rural craft is where that proof is stress-tested. Read the <a href=\"#blog/physician-retention-3-year-kpi\" data-go=\"blog/physician-retention-3-year-kpi\">retention pillar</a>.</p></details>\n<details><summary>What\u2019s the client next step?</summary><p><button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">Open the client path</button> \u2014 facility type includes FQHC / community health \u2014 then hiring preview and meeting request.</p></details>\n<details><summary>What\u2019s the physician next step?</summary><p><button class=\"linkish\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">Search for the right role</button> or open a practice-first job preview from the physician path.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m hiring for a community / FQHC seat</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"physician\" data-trail=\"1\">I\u2019m a physician exploring community roles</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"blog/interview-to-placement-efficiency\">Read the 1.7 efficiency pillar</button></p>\n<h2>Close the loop to a recruiting guide / hiring guide</h2>\n<p>World-class rural recruiting content fails if it ends in a PDF. AMP\u2019s education clickthrough keeps Education \u2192 doors \u2192 preview \u2192 Tap to Talk / Request meeting \u2192 a recruiting guide or hiring guide. Sixteen years since 2010, 700+ partnerships, 1.7 interview efficiency, and 87% retention are operating proof — use them together.</p>\n<p>If this guide helped, act inside five minutes. Community patients do not benefit from bookmarked articles alone \u2014 they benefit when a prepared candidate meets a prepared client ready to hire. Keep linking Education to doors: dedicated investment vs contingent for model choice, retention for the stay-rate KPI, and interview efficiency for hiring-meeting hygiene. Rural and FQHC leaders who want a meeting should say facility type plainly on the client path; physicians who want community pace should filter specialty and region, open a practice-first preview when it fits, and Tap to Talk so a recruiting guide owns the thread.</p>\n"
,
"amp-history-16-years": "\n<p>Sixteen years is not a slogan. It is Adaptive Medical Partners’ public clock: founded in 2010 to specialize in healthcare recruiting for underserved communities — that was the whole focus, not a side practice. This history is the About Us timeline in retention voice. No invented milestones. No trophy wall.</p>\n<p>AMP still publishes the same public proof beside that tenure: <strong>87% retention at three years</strong>, <strong>1.7 average interviews per successful placement</strong>, and partnerships with <strong>700+ rural hospitals / FQHCs / critical access facilities</strong>. The years explain how those numbers were earned.</p>\n<h2>2010 — the mission begins</h2>\n<p>AMP was founded to recruit for underserved communities. Candidates search for the right role. Clients arrive ready to hire. AMP prepares both sides so the hiring meeting is informed.</p>\n<h2>2011 — text outreach and Doximity</h2>\n<p>In 2011 AMP became the first physician recruiting firm to text busy physicians instead of leaving voicemails. The same year AMP was the first recruiting firm to partner with Doximity as physician membership was still emerging — before Doximity membership surpassed the AMA.</p>\n<h2>2015–2018 — growth recognition</h2>\n<p>AMP was named to the Bulldog 100 (fastest-growing UGA-alumni businesses) and the Inc. 5000. Three consecutive years on each list, including #25 on the Bulldog 100 in 2018.</p>\n<h2>2017 — Sirius XM Doctor Radio</h2>\n<p>An exclusive partnership with Sirius XM’s Doctor Radio — the only recruiting firm with that physician-audience access.</p>\n<h2>2018 — integrated marketing</h2>\n<p>AMP launched cross-channel marketing to reach passive physician candidates through multiple touchpoints at once, without lowering the bar for committed physicians.</p>\n<h2>2020 — AMP NOW</h2>\n<p>AMP launched a free telemedicine platform so healthcare organizations could respond to patients in the early days of the pandemic — support beyond recruitment.</p>\n<h2>2021–2022 — matching and admin tools</h2>\n<p>In 2021 AMP launched AI-powered candidate matching (Metropolis) so every candidate presented has a higher chance of lasting success. In 2022 AMP adopted AI admin tools that increased recruiter efficiency while keeping the personal approach to physician recruiting.</p>\n<h2>2025–2026 — boutique return, AMP reborn</h2>\n<p>In 2025, under full ownership by founder David Fontenot, AMP refocused as a boutique, client-centric firm — proven methods plus modern tools. The 2026 chapter is a rebirth: AI is now part of how the firm guides each search — reading the market more clearly — while the boutique craft stays accountable through the hire.</p>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details open><summary>Where does this timeline come from?</summary><p>Only from AMP’s public About Us evolution timeline (2010–2026). This page does not add awards, towns, or KPIs beyond that record and the locked public proof numbers.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Where should I read the years in order?</summary><p>Open <a href=\"#about\" data-go=\"about\">About</a> for the full evolution list, then return here or to Why AMP.</p></details>\n<details><summary>I’m hiring — what’s the next step?</summary><p>Enter the client door, share facility type and specialty, tell us what you need help with, and request a meeting with a hiring guide.</p></details>\n</div>\n<p><button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-go=\"about\">Read the About timeline</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"education\">Why AMP</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\" data-go=\"client\" data-trail=\"1\">I’m hiring — hold a clear brief</button></p>\n",
"first-job-shortfalls-residents-fellows": `<p>Coming out of training feels like a milestone. The first offer can look complete because the fatigue is real and the next chapter is waiting. AMP’s job is not to push you toward a role; it is to help you see the search conditions before you commit.</p><p>As reported by the AMA, nearly 60% of physicians leave their first post-training job within three years, and more than 25% consider leaving within the first year. Those numbers are a prompt for better questions, not a forecast for your future.</p><h2>Shortfall one: the week is a slogan</h2><p>Ask someone to walk you through their typical week. When does clinic end? Who covers when the schedule runs long? How often is call truly shared? “Collegial” is not a schedule. A clear week is a place to start.</p><h2>Shortfall two: place is treated as scenery</h2><p>Place is not a hero image. It is partner work, school timing, family distance, community fit, and the version of yourself you can sustain there. Read our <a href="#blog/place-first-without-regret" data-go="blog/place-first-without-regret">place-first guide</a> before you rank a region.</p><h2>Shortfall three: contract literacy arrives late</h2><p>The AMA points physicians toward retention questions, practice longevity and affiliations, and contract literacy. Ask what changes after any guarantee, how production is measured, what termination means, and who will explain the language without rushing you.</p><h2>Shortfall four: no second-move plan</h2><p>A first job is important, not irreversible — and moving again is hard. Put a two-year check on your calendar: week, support, leadership, economics, and your own energy. Use it to name what is working, fix what is not, and give clear feedback to your employer while the relationship can still improve. Staying put is usually better than another search; the two-year check is how you stay in a problem-solving mindset so you are more likely to keep a good seat (and help make it better). If the fit is truly wrong after that work, a thoughtful second move still beats a quiet third year of regret.</p><p>AMP is the guide, not the headline. Use the physician path when you are ready, or send a short note to a named guide. Full packages belong in a conversation; your questions belong at the start.</p>`,
"place-first-without-regret": `<p>Specialty is the starting point. Place is the weather, pace, people, and life around the practice. Residents and fellows often start with a job title and only later ask whether the place can hold the life they want. AMP helps reverse that order without pretending a region has one story.</p><h2>Define place in plain terms</h2><p>Write down what your household needs: partner work, school rhythm, family distance, airport access, outdoors, culture, and the kind of community where you want to be known. None of those answers is universal, and none needs a made-up town story.</p><h2>Then test the practice</h2><p>Place-first does not mean practice-last. Ask how the week fits the life you just described. A beautiful corridor cannot rescue a call schedule that breaks the promises you made at home. A strong team cannot erase a place your family cannot inhabit.</p><h2>Use regions as questions, not verdicts</h2><p>AMP’s region map is a starting point. Select more than one zone, compare the language, and ask a guide what is known versus what still needs a call. MGMA can provide compensation and practice context; associations and public benchmarks are useful context, not a personal offer.</p><h2>Make the hiring meeting conversation concrete</h2><ul><li>What does an ordinary Tuesday look like?</li><li>What support exists when volume or call spikes?</li><li>What affiliations and referral relationships shape the practice?</li><li>What would make you still want to be there in two years?</li></ul><p>Place-first without regret is not about finding a perfect postcard. It is about choosing a place whose trade-offs you can name. When you want help, AMP can guide the next question—and keep the full map off the public page.</p>`,
"two-year-test-before-you-sign": `<p>A contract can be accurate and still fail to answer the question that matters: can I live this job for two years? Before you sign, AMP recommends a simple audit. It is an education tool, not legal advice; have counsel review the document.</p><h2>Test the week</h2><p>Compare written hours, call, clinic volume, inpatient obligations, and coverage to the week described in interviews. Ask what happens during holidays, vacancies, and growth. If the schedule depends on everyone being fully staffed forever, you have found a risk worth naming.</p><h2>Test the economics</h2><p>Understand guarantee length, wRVU or other production mechanics, conversion points, bonuses, repayment language, and expenses. MGMA compensation benchmarks can be context for a conversation; they are not a promise for your specialty or place.</p><h2>Test the relationship</h2><p>Who answers when a contract question appears? Ask how leadership handles feedback, how physicians learn about change, and whether the team can describe practice longevity and affiliations. The AMA encourages physicians to ask retention questions when evaluating first jobs.</p><h2>Test the exit</h2><p>Read termination, notice, restrictive covenant, malpractice tail, and repayment sections slowly. A guide can help you build questions; counsel should advise you on rights and obligations.</p><h2>Keep your second move visible</h2><p>Put a two-year check-in on your calendar before the first day. Score the week, support, contract reality, place, and energy. You are not planning to leave; you are keeping agency. If the first role needs a second move, you will know why.</p><p>Ready to compare a role? AMP’s public pages stay preview-level. Tap to Talk when a practice earns the next conversation.</p>`,
"mpc-when-location-leads": `<p>Sometimes location leads. A resident has a partner constraint, a family corridor, or a clear picture of the place they can call home before the practice is known. That is a real filter. It is also where the MPC contrast can be useful.</p><h2>MPC vs contingent contrast</h2><p>AMP uses MPC as a contrast: a candidate explores an open search without a dedicated seat yet, while a contingent client often watches the market from a distance. It is a different path, not a promise of live inventory.</p><h2>When the location filter helps</h2><p>Use place as a first cut when it protects a real household need. Then ask whether the practice can support the life you are protecting. A location can lead the conversation without becoming the entire decision.</p><h2>When a guide adds value</h2><p>Dedicated investment search is the dedicated path: you are searching for the right role, the client is ready to hire with a clear brief, and AMP does the search work between them. A guide can translate vague place language into questions about schedule, affiliations, support, and longevity.</p><p>There is no need to expose client names, search IDs, or a full MI file to learn whether a path deserves attention. Start with a Market Intelligence snapshot, then contact a guide if the example raises a real question. Preview → contact is the right sequence.</p>`,
"coordinator-share-pack-grads": `<p>Program coordinators can give graduating residents and fellows a short pack before the first job search. The goal is not to endorse a recruiter or a town. It is to help a new physician ask questions early.</p><h2>Share these five prompts</h2><ol><li>What does the ordinary week look like?</li><li>How does call work when the team is short?</li><li>What changes after the guarantee or first contract period?</li><li>How does the organization support practice longevity?</li><li>What would make this place sustainable for two years?</li></ol><h2>Point them to neutral context</h2><p>AMA reporting on early-career retention and contract literacy is a useful starting point. AAPPR 2025 benchmarking and MGMA association resources can add industry context; neither can choose a first job for an individual graduate.</p><h2>Keep the handoff personal</h2><p>Encourage graduates to compare specialty, region, place, week, and contract. If they want help, a short conversation with a guide is more useful than a full public dossier. AMP’s residents hub offers rank, region, and an illustrative MI Lite peek before the next step.</p><p>Graduation is a milestone, not the end of learning. Give each new physician a map, a checklist, and permission to ask one more question.</p>`,

"virtual-interview-how-to": `<h2>How To Do a Virtual Interview – Physician Edition</h2>
<ol>
<li>Treat it as a real interview. Dress professionally, just like you would for an in-person interview.</li>
<li>Use a computer. If you must use a phone, make sure it’s on a stand or somewhere stable.</li>
<li>Get in a well-lit area with a non-distracting background.</li>
<li>Be present and focused.</li>
<li>Turn off all alerts on your device – it’s distracting for both parties.</li>
<li>Research the organization and the person you're interviewing with ahead of time. If you’re working with a recruiting firm, your recruiter contact can help you prep!</li>
<li>Ask questions. It shows that you’re interested!</li>
<li>Make sure that your device is fully charged and that all updates have been completed.</li>
<li>For better sound quality, try using headphones or a microphone attachment.</li>
<li>Be sure that you are in an area with a strong Wi-Fi connection and that you have downloaded any necessary applications ahead of time.</li>
</ol>
<p>Most importantly, approach every interview as if you want an offer.</p>
<h2>How To Do a Virtual Interview – Organization Edition</h2>
<ol>
<li>Provide the candidate with a tailored experience based on their interests and motivations.</li>
<li>Know the goal of your interview – is to get an offer out and contract signed? Or is it more like a pre-interview phone call?</li>
<li>Set up their virtual interview to mirror your traditional interview process as much as possible.</li>
<li>Build out the interview schedule just like you would an onsite visit, with scheduled meetings throughout the day.</li>
<li>Try to avoid more than 2 people on a call if possible.</li>
<li>Have a Community Representative—someone who would normally do your community tour and answer the candidate’s questions about schools, homes, and amenities.</li>
<li>If you must do a large group call, be sure to delegate a moderator to keep the conversation going and/or prevent overtalking.</li>
<li>Make sure all participants in the call are prepared with questions and dialogue.</li>
</ol>
<p>Remember that you are selling them just as much as they are selling you.</p>`,
"rural-recruiting-keeping-candidates-open-minded": `<p>As a permanent physician recruiting firm, we talk daily with candidates and get down to the nitty-gritty of what motivates them to consider new opportunities.</p>
<p>Many of our clients are in rural communities and we often get asked… how do we communicate with candidates to determine if they are open to considering a move to a rural practice?</p>
<p>According to the AAMC, only 11% of physicians are practicing in rural areas. So, it stands to reason that most physicians focus their search on larger metropolitan areas because they are more familiar with these locations, either by personal experience or name recognition. This doesn’t mean that a more rural location wouldn’t be a good fit for them, it just means they need to be informed about what a rural location can offer them. Determining whether a candidate would be interested in your rural opportunity starts with learning about the physician!</p>
<p>Instead of asking a candidate if they are interested in a rural area, focus the conversation on talking points that have nothing to do with “Zip Code".</p>
<p>Try asking…</p>
<ul>
<li>What is important to them in a community?</li>
<li>What does their spouse enjoy doing when they are not working?</li>
<li>Do they have children, and if so, what activities would a community need to offer to keep them happy?</li>
<li>What do they enjoy doing as a family on weekends, but also what do they actually have time to do?</li>
<li>What are the candidate’s personal hobbies or interests outside of medicine?</li>
</ul>
<p>It’s important to remember that most physicians won’t have a rural location in mind when they start their job search – in fact, some will even be turned off by the prospect of moving to a rural location. Therefore, you should use language that emphasizes the experience and positives of what your community has to offer the candidate based on their specific values and interests, just to open their mind. Avoid using language that emphasizes the remoteness of the location. (“There are two airports within an hour drive of our location” is much more appealing than “There aren’t any major airports in our town”).</p>
<p>Once you’ve learned what is important to a physician, you can then determine whether they might be a good fit for your facility and community. Oftentimes, the needs of the physician and their family can be met in a rural area, they just need a little help in seeing it!</p>
<p>Almost certainly your practice opportunity is significantly better than anything they will find in a big city, so that is your hook. The goal should always be to gain interest from the candidate in things about your facility - organizational goals, personalities of your staff, and their ideal professional goals. Sell the practice as the one thing that meets their professional needs, and just open their mind on the community. The goal is to get them onsite, and then woo them with that small town hospitality when you have all of their attention focused on you!</p>`,
"can-you-zoom-to-success-in-physician-recruiting": `<h2>The role of virtual interviews in the physician recruitment process</h2>
<p>Virtual technology has Zoomed into ubiquity over the past two years, infiltrating all aspects of life and work, including the physician recruiting industry. Now that social distancing and pandemic-related restrictions are (for the most part) in the rearview mirror, should virtual interviews still be a part of the standard physician recruiting process?</p>
<p>It depends.</p>
<p>“We do think that there is a place for the virtual interview in physician recruiting, if utilized and executed strategically,” said Tim Ketterman, VP of Operations for Adaptive Medical Partners. “Remote interviews can be helpful sometimes, depending upon the location and recruiting needs of the employer, and could help to streamline the interview process especially for employers located in areas that are more frequently sought out by physician candidates.”</p>
<p>Dan Jones, Senior Director of Recruiting, agrees, based upon his ongoing experience with physician candidates and employers. In general, Jones has noticed a decline in the focus on the candidate experience overall, perhaps because healthcare leaders are so busy juggling so many other demanding challenges lately. However, is convenience ultimately worth sacrificing results? While employers may be in contact with more candidates via virtual technology, they are having fewer meaningful, productive onsite interviews.</p>
<p>“We've seen a 40% increase in our conversion rate of Candidate Presentations to Client Submission for Interview Consideration, but a 20% decrease in the conversion of those client submissions turning into actual onsite interviews,” said Jones, adding that the indication from candidate feedback is that this is due to lackluster virtual interviews.</p>
<h2>Make Memories, Not Phone Calls</h2>
<p>“The interview should be a comprehensive, memorable ‘experience’ for the physician and his or her family,” Jones added. “When recruiting a physician, employers must do everything they can to differentiate themselves from other organizations and communities."</p>
<p>A one- or two-hour transactional virtual interview is not sufficient to convey all that an employer and a community have to offer, nor does it enable ample opportunities for the physician to form a connection with potential colleagues.</p>
<p>“In a remote interview, many employers are neglecting to leverage one of the best recruiting assets: their people,” said Jones. “Bringing a doctor on-site for an interview allows the candidate to experience the team’s engagement and rapport first-hand,” and for the staff members to also engage with the physician candidate, Jones explained. “These experiences cannot be shared via a Zoom call, at least not in a meaningful way. They must be a lived experienced – bring someone onsite for a day to allow them to get a feel for what life is really like as part of your team and your organization.”</p>
<p>This is especially true for employers in rural areas that would require a physician to relocate to a smaller community. “Recruitment that requires relocation to a rural area automatically makes you a runner-up about 90% of the time, to employers in larger cities,” said Jones. “Many candidates haven’t lived in rural areas, so they don’t truly understand the numerous benefits of living and practicing in a smaller community. Fear of the unknown is a real phenomenon – most candidates naturally gravitate towards the familiar – when making a big life decision, most will go with what they know.” Therefore, if there’s only a virtual interview upon which to base the decision, relocating to a rural area is an easy “no” for candidates, without the emotional and experiential impact of an onsite interview.</p>
<p>Utilizing remote interviews also gives employers a false sense of security in their recruiting process, with seemingly greater candidate activity. However, conducting a higher volume of virtual interviews doesn’t lead to increased success in filling more physician openings, Ketterman has found.</p>
<p>“Are clients getting a chance to talk to more candidates by utilizing remote interviews? Probably so, but that’s only because the bar is set lower – i.e. the qualifications are fewer - for a virtual interview than for an onsite visit,” Ketterman explained. While clients may feel as if they are gaining the opportunity to evaluate more candidates by utilizing virtual interviews, that’s likely not the reality. “I’d say that when interviewing remotely, employers are actually talking to more applicants, rather than interviewing more candidates who are truly qualified and realistically interested and invested in the opportunity.”</p>
<h2>No Pain, No Gain?</h2>
<p>“Sure, it’s more convenient to jump on a call or a Zoom meeting than to set up travel, accommodations, and an itinerary for an onsite interview,” Jones added, and candidates know it too. As a prospective employer, what message do you want to convey to the physician you want and need to join your organization? “Employers must roll out the red carpet to make the candidate and his or her family feel wanted in the community and the organization,” Jones continued.</p>
<p>Investing in the time and expense to bring a candidate onsite for an interview shows more engagement and interest from the employer. “An onsite interview is more than just a ‘check-the-box’ meeting or a quick vetting call. Onsite interviews demonstrate the employer’s interest and investment in the candidate, which is attractive and helps form a mutual emotional connection.”</p>
<p>Jones recognizes that putting your organization and your staff out there for an onsite meeting doesn’t come without some risk. “In addition to offering convenience, virtual interviews offer protection from some of the financial, and quite frankly, the emotional risk too. Recruiting requires full engagement, and it can be disappointing to expend the energy and time, only to be ultimately turned down by a candidate,” he said.</p>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>While virtual technology can be a helpful resource for physician recruiting in some cases, most healthcare employers cannot successfully rely on remote interviews for the best recruiting results. Depending too heavily on virtual interviews can be the fastest way for a healthcare employer to Zoom out of consideration among physician candidates, and right into a physician staffing crisis.</p>`,
"how-does-your-physician-search-stack-up-against-the-competition": `<p>How fillable is your physician search? This unique scoring system can tell you exactly how your search compares.</p>
<p>Each day your physician search remains unfilled costs your group, hospital, or health system thousands of dollars in lost revenue. Not only that, it adds to your workload and stress as a healthcare recruiter.</p>
<p>You already know that.</p>
<p>With industry-wide physician unemployment rates at nearly 0%, every physician staffing need becomes urgent, if for no other reason than to reach as many open candidates as possible before another organization swoops them up.</p>
<p>And because of that urgency, many recruiting departments feel forced to blast out emails, post job ads, and immediately start marketing their openings with the hopes the shotgun approach will help them screen as many qualified physician candidates as quickly as possible.</p>
<p>The results? Well, as fellow recruiters, we don’t have to tell you how frustrating the process is.</p>
<h2>Is there a better way to position your physician opportunities?</h2>
<p>While urgency is essential in physician search, how you position your search is equally, if not more, critical to the success of your search.</p>
<p>What you include in your messaging, how you position the search, and how you have conversations with physicians each impacts your search. Legendary salesperson Elmer Wheeler once taught, “Sell the sizzle, not the steak.”</p>
<p>(For an entertaining and insightful work break, <a href="https://youtu.be/x6tZsmGYvaM" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">check out this video</a> about Elmer’s sales philosophies!)</p>
<p>To know what sizzle to use, it helps to know how your search compares to the physician job market.</p>
<p>“It’s impossible to successfully market your practice opportunity if you don’t understand how to position it in the market,” said Tim Ketterman, Chief Operations Officer of Adaptive Medical Partners.</p>
<p>“In most specialties, more physician jobs are available than physicians to fill them. Therefore, you need to know how your opportunity measures up and plan your recruiting efforts accordingly,” said Ketterman.</p>
<h2>How do you know if your physician opening compares to competing physician jobs?</h2>
<p>The AMP Score™ online scoring tool is a convenient way to help hospital leaders, healthcare administrators, and recruiters understand how their physician opportunity compares to the competition.</p>
<p>When comparing your opportunity to competing opportunities in the market, the AMP Score™ evaluates your position against the following competitive factors:</p>
<ul>
<li>Position Location</li>
<li>Position Compensation</li>
<li>Position Quality of Life / Livability Index</li>
<li>Quality of Practice Index</li>
<li>Interview and Decision-Making Factors</li>
</ul>
<p>Over the last 12 years, Adaptive Medical Partners has found these five are the key drivers of physicians’ career decisions when comparing multiple practice opportunities and offers.</p>
<p>The AMP Score™ involves a simple multiple-choice questionnaire that takes hospital leaders, healthcare administrators, and recruiters 3-5 minutes to complete.</p>
<p>The AMP Score™ will then provide you with your competitive job score and comparisons to other states, regional, and national AMP Score™ results in the current job market.</p>
<p>Additionally, the AMP Score™ will help you understand other factors to use in your physician communications, including:</p>
<ul>
<li>Area school rankings</li>
<li>Airport access</li>
<li>Access to beaches, mountains, lakes, etc.</li>
<li>Metropolitan size and amenities</li>
<li>Compensation and financial perks</li>
<li>Professional perks</li>
<li>Interview process and length of the cycle for feedback and offers</li>
<li>Job opportunities for spouse / other family members</li>
</ul>
<p>“With the mobility of professionals in today’s workforce, health systems are now competing nationally for top physician talent – not just within your city, state, or region,” Ketterman added.</p>
<h2>Get Your AMP Score™ – FREE!</h2>
<p>As fellow recruiters engaged in the daily fight, we are providing the AMP Score™ tool free of charge and with no obligation. Just click <a href="https://www.getampscore.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">here</a> to get your score.</p>
<h2>OK, OK… I’ve got my AMP Score™. What do I do next?</h2>
<p>“If your AMP score is at or below the comps, you may need to adjust your practice offering, if possible,” Ketterman advised. “While you can’t control your community amenities or location, you can adjust the model, improve your interview process, add perks, or increase compensation to increase your AMP Score. Sometimes one big obvious change can help, but often it requires pulling multiple levers to impact how the candidate market views your opportunity.”</p>
<p>Once you’ve adjusted your practice offerings, if your AMP Score is still low, plan to invest more time and resources into your search, including potentially hiring a search firm for assistance.</p>
<p>“Physician recruiting is highly labor-intensive, and that’s especially true for certain specialties and jobs that are tough to fill and have a difficult time attracting candidates through traditional methods,” Ketterman explained. “Therefore, it’s generally more cost-effective to outsource recruiting to a reputable firm rather than tie up your staff and resources with a time-consuming, laborious physician search that could drag on for months or years without professional assistance.”</p>
<p>Ketterman continues, “The AMP Score™ analysis helps you and our team identify potential weaknesses in your opportunity so that we can work around them, but it can also help us identify strengths to promote. We’d be happy to sit down with anyone to discuss their score in detail, regardless of whether you choose to partner with us.”</p>
<p>To learn your AMP Score today for any physician opening, visit <a href="https://www.getampscore.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">www.GetAMPScore.com</a>.</p>`
};
